Q: Pass data to child components that are multiple levels deep A: <p>Passing as inputs has a limitation, when the number of inputs are large, or the inputs must be passed through multiple levels to reach the target component. A simple way to solve this is to use a service for data sharing.</p>
<p>Store the parent variables inside a service. Ensure you add the service to the parent component, so that when the component is destroyed the service also gets destroyed.</p>
<pre><code>@Component({
  selector: 'app-parent',
  ...
  providers: [SharedService],
})
...
</code></pre>
<p>Ensure the service does not have <code>providedIn: 'root'</code> since we want the scope to be at the component level.</p>
<pre><code>@Injectable()
export class SharedService {
  a: any;
  b: any;
  c: any;
  d: any;
  e: any;
  f: any;
  g: any;
  ...
}
</code></pre>
<p>Use this service through dependency injection in the child components.</p>
<pre><code>@Component({
  selector: 'app-child2-child1',
  ...
})
export class Child2Child1 {
  private sharedService = inject(SharedService);

  constructor() {
    console.log(this.sharedService.a);
  }

  ...
}
</code></pre>

Q: How can I define different style for different page in CSS? A: <p>Add Page-Specific Classes to the Body Tag</p>
<p>For login.html:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;body class=&quot;login-page&quot;&gt;
  &lt;!-- content --&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>For signup.html:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;body class=&quot;signup-page&quot;&gt;
  &lt;!-- content --&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>For other-page.html:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;body class=&quot;dashboard-page&quot;&gt;
  &lt;!-- content --&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>And in style.css:</p>
<pre><code>body {
  font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
}

/* Login page specific styles */
.login-page {
  background: linear-gradient(135deg, #667eea 0%, #764ba2 100%);
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;
  align-items: center;
  min-height: 100vh;
}

/* Signup page specific styles */
.signup-page {
  background: linear-gradient(135deg, #f093fb 0%, #f5576c 100%);
  min-height: 100vh;
}

/* Dashboard page specific styles */
.dashboard-page {
  background: #f5f5f5;
}
</code></pre>
<p>This is industry standart way and it is easy to maintain. You don't need to create divs with 100vh/100vw— just style the body element differently based on its class.</p>
